{
  "teamId": 121,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 New York Mets arrived with championship expectations and largely delivered on them, surging to 101 wins behind a star-studded roster, elite pitching, and a relentless offensive attack. From the opening weeks of April, when the lineup sparked and the bullpen occasionally stumbled, to the blazing summer months that saw the Mets build one of the best records in baseball, this was a team that refused to quit even when its highest-leverage moments cut against it. Edwin Díaz and the relief corps had rough nights in May, and the Giants torched the Mets bullpen in a memorable late-May collapse, but the wins kept piling up because the offense — led by Pete Alonso, Francisco Lindor, and the newly acquired Starling Marte — consistently answered the bell.\n\nThe heart of the season was defined by resilience. July brought a dominant 17-8 stretch, punctuated by clutch doubles from Marte and a heads-up rally sparked by a Tomas Nido double and a wild throwing error. August was similarly dominant at 19-11, featuring a pair of enormous Mark Canha moments and a Pete Alonso single that capped a thrilling comeback against Colorado. Lindor's grand slam in September encapsulated what this team was about: big swings in big moments, a shortstop finally living up to the mammoth contract New York had given him. The Mets won games in every way imaginable, grinding out victories in pitchers' duels, slugging their way through lineups, and finding heroes in unlikely places like Nick Plummer's pinch-hit homer in late May.\n\nThe postseason, however, told a different and cruel story. The Wild Card round against the San Diego Padres exposed the Mets' vulnerabilities in concentrated, brutal fashion. Max Scherzer was rocked for two home runs in Game 1, with Josh Bell and Trent Grisham doing the damage, and Jacob deGrom — so long the ace everyone hoped would lead a Mets October — couldn't hold the lead in Game 2 as Grisham homered again and Jurickson Profar drove in another run. The Mets clawed with a Lindor solo shot and an Alonso blast, but it was never quite enough. Chris Bassitt's Game 3 implosion — allowing two runs on an Austin Nola single that broke open the decisive game — closed the book on the season.\n\nThe 2022 Mets were a genuinely excellent baseball team, one of the best the franchise had produced in years, and yet they will be remembered as much for how it ended as for the 101 wins that preceded October. A 13-12 June hinted at the cracks that the postseason would ruthlessly expose. The bullpen, the one area that never quite locked in for a full sustained stretch, failed at the worst moments. For Mets fans, the sting of a first-round exit after a 101-win season is the lasting memory — a reminder that in baseball, a great regular season is only a prologue.",
